    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-4.1.07-06",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "A scatter graph plots paired quantitative observations, one variable on each axis. It can show positive, negative or little apparent association and exceptions. Points should retain their pairs; rearranging each variable independently destroys the relationship. Correlation does not isolate causation. Visitor counts and path widths can be associated while slope, construction and earlier use also contribute to the form. A line of best fit drawn through the middle of the points, with roughly as many points above as below, shows the general trend; points far from it are anomalies worth explaining.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-4.1.07-1",
        "CL-6137-4.1.07-2",
